Lab 19: Integrating Camel with External Data Sources
Objectives
Configure Apache Camel to interact with external databases (SQL and NoSQL)
Set up file system integration for reading/writing data
Implement data transformations between CSV, JSON, and XML formats
Understand Camel's component model for data integration
Prerequisites
Java JDK 11+ installed
Apache Maven project set up
Basic understanding of Apache Camel
Access to a terminal/command prompt
Docker installed (for database containers)
Setup Requirements
Create a new Maven project:
mvn archetype:generate -DgroupId=com.camel.lab -DartifactId=camel-data-integration -DarchetypeArtifactId=maven-archetype-quickstart -DinteractiveMode=false
Add Camel dependencies to pom.xml:
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-sql</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-jackson</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-csv</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
Lab Tasks
Task 1: File System Integration
Subtask 1.1: Reading from Files
Create a route to read from a directory:
from("file:input?noop=true")
    .log("File content: ${body}")
    .to("file:output");
Create a sample input file input/test.txt with some sample content.
Expected Outcome: The file should be copied to the output directory with the same content.

Subtask 1.2: Writing to Files
Modify the route to transform content before writing:
from("file:input?noop=true")
    .transform(body().append("\nProcessed at: ").append(header("CamelFileName")))
    .to("file:output");
Expected Outcome: Output files will contain original content plus processing metadata.

Task 2: Database Integration
Subtask 2.1: SQL Database Setup
Start a PostgreSQL container:
docker run --name camel-postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=password -p 5432:5432 -d postgres
Add PostgreSQL dependency:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
    <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
    <version>42.6.0</version>
</dependency>
Create a route to insert data:
from("timer:dbTimer?period=10000")
    .setBody(constant("INSERT INTO users(name, email) VALUES ('John', 'john@example.com')"))
    .to("jdbc:dataSource");
Configure datasource in application.properties:
camel.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/postgres
camel.datasource.username=postgres
camel.datasource.password=password
Expected Outcome: Every 10 seconds, a new record is inserted into the database.

Subtask 2.2: NoSQL Integration (MongoDB)
Start MongoDB container:
docker run --name camel-mongo -p 27017:27017 -d mongo
Add MongoDB dependencies:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
    <artifactId>camel-mongodb</artifactId>
    <version>3.20.0</version>
</dependency>
Create a MongoDB route:
from("direct:insertMongo")
    .setBody(constant("{name: 'Jane', email: 'jane@example.com'}"))
    .to("mongodb:myDb?database=test&collection=users&operation=insert");
Expected Outcome: Document is inserted into MongoDB when route is triggered.

Task 3: Data Transformation
Subtask 3.1: CSV to JSON
Create a sample CSV file input/data.csv:
id,name,email
1,John,john@example.com
2,Jane,jane@example.com
Create transformation route:
from("file:input?fileName=data.csv&noop=true")
    .unmarshal().csv()
    .marshal().json()
    .to("file:output?fileName=data.json");
Expected Outcome: JSON file created with equivalent data structure.

Subtask 3.2: XML to JSON
Add XML data format dependency:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
    <artifactId>camel-jaxb</artifactId>
    <version>3.20.0</version>
</dependency>
Create transformation route:
from("file:input?noop=true")
    .unmarshal().jacksonXml()
    .marshal().json()
    .to("file:output");
Troubleshooting Tips
If database connections fail, verify container is running and credentials are correct
For file operations, check directory permissions
Enable Camel debug logging for detailed route tracing
For transformation issues, verify the input data matches expected format
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

Configured Camel to interact with file systems
Integrated with both SQL and NoSQL databases
Implemented data transformations between common formats
Gained practical experience with Camel's component model
These skills form the foundation for building robust data integration solutions using Apache Camel.
